<date date-type="accepted"><day>18</day><month>2</month><year>2025</year></date>
</history><permissions><!--&#x000a9; 2025 Shandong Cancer Hospital & Institute.--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). <italic toggle="yes">Precision Radiation Oncology</italic> published by John Wiley &#x00026; Sons Australia, Ltd on behalf of Shandong Cancer Hospital &#x00026; Institute.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited and is not used for commercial purposes.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:PRO6-9-61.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><p>The approach to prophylactic neck irradiation in nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) has undergone significant changes. For decades, prophylactic whole&#x02010;neck irradiation has been the standard for all patients with NPC; however, it is linked to a high risk of late complications. Advances in imaging modalities have deepened understanding of the metastatic characteristics of the cervical lymph nodes (LN), prompting a shift towards sparing the uninvolved lower neck and medial retropharyngeal nodal region. This targeted approach has proven effective in controlling cervical LN recurrence as whole&#x02010;neck irradiation while significantly reducing adverse effects. Currently, contouring of the neck lymphatic drainage clinical target volume (CTV) is being explored to eliminate the use of uniform cervical LN levels as a delineation boundary. Instead, the inferior boundary of the neck CTV is determined either by the distance from the lowest positive LN or two cervical vertebrae below the lowest positive LN, facilitating more individualized CTV delineation and prophylactic neck irradiation. Additionally, omitting lower&#x02010;risk neck lymphatic drainage CTVs and irradiating only visible LNs in these areas are also being explored. This review examines the evolution of prophylactic neck irradiation for NPC, providing key insights into these advancements.</p></abstract><abstract id="pro670007-abs-0001" abstract-type="graphical"><p>This paper summarizes the evolving developments of prophylactic neck irradiation for NPC and provides important insights and key evidence from historical, current, and recent studies.
<boxed-text position="anchor" content-type="graphic"><graphic xlink:href="PRO6-9-61-g001.jpg" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></boxed-text>
</p></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="pro670007-kwd-0001">head and neck squamous cell carcinoma</kwd><kwd id="pro670007-kwd-0002">nasopharyngeal carcinoma</kwd><kwd id="pro670007-kwd-0003">neck irradiation</kwd><kwd id="pro670007-kwd-0004">radiotherapy</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group id="funding-0001"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>National Natural Science Foundation of China</institution><institution-id institution-id-type="doi">10.13039/501100001809</institution-id></institution-wrap>
</funding-source><award-id>82203197</award-id></award-group></funding-group><counts><fig-count count="2"/><table-count count="1"/><page-count count="8"/><word-count count="6555"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>March 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.4 mode:remove_FC converted:28.10.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><p content-type="self-citation">
<mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="pro670007-cite-0001">
<string-name>
<surname>Liao</surname>
<given-names>W</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Zhao</surname>
<given-names>Y</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Zhang</surname>
<given-names>S</given-names>
</string-name>. <article-title>The evolution of prophylactic neck irradiation in nasopharyngeal carcinoma: Changing concepts and irradiation ranges</article-title>. <source>Prec Radiat Oncol</source>. <year>2025</year>;<volume>9</volume>:<fpage>61</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>68</lpage>. <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/pro6.70007</pub-id>
</mixed-citation>
</p><fn-group><fn id="pro670007-note-0001" fn-type="equal"><p>Wenjun Liao and Yue Zhao contributed equally to this work.</p></fn></fn-group></notes></front><body><sec id="pro670007-sec-0010"><label>1</label><title>BACKGROUND</title><p>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), a distinct subtype of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), is characterized by a highly uneven geographical distribution. In 2018, approximately 70% of the estimated 130,000 global cases were concentrated in Southeast Asia (including Southern China) and northern Africa.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> Owing to the dense regional lymphatic network, cervical lymph node (LN) metastasis is highly prevalent in NPC, affecting over 70% of the patients at initial diagnosis. Given NPC's anatomical location and radiosensitivity, radiotherapy (RT) remains the primary treatment modality, targeting both the primary tumor and metastatic cervical LNs.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>For decades, prophylactic irradiation of the entire bilateral neck has been the standard approach to cervical LN management in NPC, irrespective of nodal metastasis.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref> Although this method has provided benefits, more radiation&#x02010;associated toxicities occur, with up to 40% of patients developing hypothyroidism and 39% experiencing dysphagia.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref> Other late complications, such as tissue necrosis and cervical fibrosis, are also prevalent.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Favorable treatment outcomes coupled with significant late toxicities in NPC have driven interest in de&#x02010;escalating treatment strategies. Given that the extent of normal tissue injury and the incidence of late complications are closely linked to radiation dose and volume, reducing the extent of prophylactic neck irradiation has become a key focus in RT research. Currently, the transition from whole&#x02010;neck irradiation (WNI) to uninvolved lower&#x02010;neck&#x02010;sparing irradiation and individualized irradiation has significantly reduced the overall irradiated volume of the neck. These improvements have led to a significant decrease in complications in patients with NPC; however, survival rates remain unaffected.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>5</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>This review summarizes the latest advancements in prophylactic neck irradiation for NPC, offering a comprehensive and critical analysis of this specialized domain.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0020"><label>2</label><title>METHODOLOGY</title><p>This narrative review was conducted by selectively searching PubMed and examining the reference lists of relevant studies. The search prioritized studies related to prophylactic neck irradiation in patients with NPC. Three authors (LWJ, ZY, and SCZ) reviewed the full text.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0030"><label>3</label><title>PROPHYLACTIC WNI: PROPOSAL AND ADOPTION</title><p>In HNSCC, &#x0201c;prophylaxis&#x0201d; usually refers to radical neck dissection or neck irradiation without clinically detectable LNs.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref> In 1950, Hayes Martin conducted a survey among surgeons on the treatment of head and neck cancer. Most participants indicated that a 10% risk of recurrence in the neck should prompt prophylactic neck dissection.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>7</sup>
</xref> This recommendation formed the basis for prophylactic neck management. However, the first report strongly advocating prophylactic WNI was published in 1963.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref> Million et&#x000a0;al. analyzed 281 patients with HNSCC, including 90 with NPC, who underwent prophylactic WNI. Notably, none of the patients with NPC developed new LNs in the irradiated region when the primary tumor and clinically palpable LNs were controlled. Based on these findings, they proposed routine WNI for NPC management, arguing that nasopharyngeal tumors are usually anaplastic and NPC is highly prone to early cervical LN metastases, both of which can be effectively controlled through prophylactic neck irradiation. However, their study lacked a control group of non&#x02010;irradiated patients, limiting direct comparison between irradiated and unirradiated cases.</p><p>Subsequently, many researchers have demonstrated the necessity of prophylactic neck irradiation for HNSCC.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref> In 1971, Berger et&#x000a0;al. assessed the efficacy of prophylactic neck irradiation for squamous cell tumors from the nasopharynx, tonsillar fossa, and tongue.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref> They reported a neck recurrence of only 2.5% (7/277) among patients who received WNI, compared with 12% (22/185) in those who underwent partial&#x02010;neck irradiation. Notably, among patients with NPC initially diagnosed with N0 disease, only 1% (1/103) treated with WNI experienced neck recurrence. They recommended WNI for patients with squamous cell tumors of the nasopharynx and oropharynx. In 1980, Rabuzzi et&#x000a0;al. conducted a survival analysis of 152 patients with clinically negative cervical LNs, further emphasizing the role of prophylactic WNI in managing subclinical neck metastases and advocating for its routine adoption by head and neck specialists.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Two retrospective studies from the Queen Elizabeth Hospital in Hong Kong further established a clinical foundation for prophylactic WNI in NPC. In 1989, Lee et&#x000a0;al. examined the treatment outcomes of 196 patients with Ho stage I NPC, of whom 189 patients did not receive WNI.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref> Among them, 30% (57/189) subsequently developed cervical LN recurrence, which was associated with higher incidence of distant metastasis and lower survival rates. However, the other seven patients who received WNI did not experience neck recurrence. In 1991, Lee et&#x000a0;al. conducted a retrospective review of 5,037 patients with NPC who received treatment between 1976 and 1995.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref> Among 1,290 patients without LN metastases, those who did not undergo WNI had a neck recurrence rate of 40% (906 patients). By contrast, the neck recurrence rate was only 11% among 384 patients who received WNI. Patients with N0 stage disease who did not undergo prophylactic WNI exhibited a higher rate of cervical LN recurrence than those who received WNI and even surpassed patients with N2 who underwent WNI. Moreover, patients who experienced neck recurrence showed a significantly higher risk of distant metastases compared with those who did not. Based on these findings and supported by those of other studies,<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref> prophylactic WNI has become a standard approach for all patients with NPC (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1A</xref>).</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="pro670007-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>The evolution of prophylactic neck irradiation in nasopharyngeal carcinoma. A patient with N1 disease (the right neck has a positive LN and the left neck has no positive LN by imaging). (A) The traditional prophylactic bilateral whole neck irradiation (WNI) (levels II, III, IV and V); (B) WNI of the right neck and upper neck irradiation (UNI) of the negative neck (levels II, III and Va). (C) In positive neck, the range of neck irradiation only covers the two vertebrae below the lowest positive LN level; In the negative neck, the range of neck irradiation only covers level II. (D) In the positive neck, the inferior boundary of the CTV is 3&#x000a0;cm below the lower edge of the positive LN; In the negative neck, the inferior boundary of the CTV is 3&#x000a0;cm below the transverse process of C1; (E) In the INRT Phase II clinical trial, they administered different doses to LNs based on risk levels, without delineating traditional CTVs. (F) In VNRT, the CTV in the positive neck includes levels II, III and Va, and visible nodes in levels IV and V (this patient has no visible nodes in these areas); In the left neck, the CTV includes level II and visible LN in Level III. LN, lymph node; CTV, clinical target volume; Ver, cervical vertebra.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="PRO6-9-61-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0040"><label>4</label><title>STUDYING THE METASTATIC CHARACTERISTICS OF CERVICAL LN: THEORETICAL BASIS FOR UNINVOLVED LOWER NECK SPARING RT</title><p>Although bilateral WNI is recommended for all patients with NPC, some scholars have differing opinions. In 1991, Sham et&#x000a0;al. examined the mode of neck involvement in 271 patients newly diagnosed with NPC at Queen Mary Hospital.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref> They found that 75.3% of patients had neck lymphadenopathy at first presentation, with the subdigastric and upper jugular groups involved in over 95% of these cases. The frequency of nodal involvement decreased progressively, with the lowest frequency observed in the neck. They hypothesized that neck involvement in NPC followed a regular and predictable progression. Consequently, they suggested that prophylactic neck irradiation should extend at least one LN level beyond clinically evident disease. Prophylactic irradiation of the upper neck alone may be considered sufficient for patients without clinically detectable LNs. However, their assessment of cervical LNs relied primarily on clinical palpation, a subjective physical examination with less sensitivity. Consequently, patients diagnosed with a negative neck may have small metastatic lesions, leading many scholars to remain cautious about partial&#x02010;neck irradiation.</p><p>However, advancements in imaging technology, such as computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and other modalities for evaluating cervical LNs, have necessitated a re&#x02010;examination of the current principles of prophylactic neck irradiation in NPC. In 1993, a study compared CT and clinical palpation to assess cervical LN metastases in NPC,<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref> reporting that CT upstaged approximately 30% of clinical N0 to N+ NPC cases. A 1997 study compared the diagnostic capabilities of CT and MRI and revealed that MRI reclassified 6% of patients with NPC from N0 (based on CT) to N+.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>22</sup>
</xref> Additionally, a 2006 study demonstrated that positron emission tomography&#x02010;CT (PET/CT) demonstrated higher sensitivity (93.2%), specificity (98.2%), and accuracy (95.4%) compared with MRI, which had corresponding values of 88.8%, 91.2%, and 89.9%.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref> Overall, these advanced imaging modalities enable a more comprehensive and accurate evaluation of cervical LNs compared with physical examination.</p><p>With the integration of advanced imaging, the characteristics of orderly progression and non&#x02010;skip metastasis of LNs in NPC have become increasingly evident. In 2008, Tang et&#x000a0;al. investigated the nodal spread modes of cervical LN in 924 patients with non&#x02010;disseminated NPC who underwent MRI.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref> They found that the retropharyngeal area (86.4%) and Level II LNs (75.1%) were the most affected sites, followed by Level III (28.8%), Level V (11.1%), Level IV (7.1%), and the supraclavicular fossa (3.9%), in descending order of involvement. Only 4 of the 786 patients (0.5%) presented with skip metastasis in their progression. A meta&#x02010;analysis conducted in 2012, involving 2,920 patients with NPC, corroborated these findings.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref> Consistent with our expectations, LN metastases followed a predictable pattern, with the incidence of skip metastases varying from 0.5% to 7.9%. These findings imply that the clinical target volume (CTVs) for prophylactic neck irradiation may be more selective than uniformly covering the entire neck.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Additionally, the recurrence patterns of the cervical LNs provided supporting evidence. In Tang's study, 138 patients with NPC and N0 disease underwent different prophylactic irradiation volumes, varying from the cricoid cartilage to the clavicle.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref> Among the patients, 37 had the cricoid cartilage (considered the caudal edge of Level III) as the lower limit of the neck irradiation field, whereas 101 had the lower border of the cricoid cartilage as the inferior boundary. No regional recurrence or significant difference in the risk of distant metastases was observed. A 2010 study of 410 patients with LN&#x02010;negative NPC who underwent upper neck irradiation with a median follow&#x02010;up duration of 54 months (Levels II, III, and VA) reported only four cases of neck recurrence, with only one patient (0.2%) experiencing a relapse outside the irradiated area.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>27</sup>
</xref> These results, along with the findings from other studies,<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>28</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref> suggest that for patients without clinically suspicious LNs, prophylactic irradiation may only be necessary at the upper neck levels.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0050"><label>5</label><title>UNINVOLVED LOWER NECK SPARING RT: EARLY EXPLORATION RESULTS</title><p>Against this background, some pioneering studies have tested this approach. In 2023, the first prospective phase II clinical study was conducted at the Jiangxi Provincial Cancer Hospital.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0030" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>30</sup>
</xref> The study randomly assigned 301 patients with NPC and negative cervical LNs into two groups: one group underwent prophylactic irradiation of the entire neck (148 patients), and the other group received prophylactic irradiation of only the upper neck (153 patients). After a median follow&#x02010;up of 39 months, no cervical LN recurrence was observed in either groups. Additionally, no significant differences were observed between the two groups regarding three&#x02010;year overall survival (OS), progression&#x02010;free survival (PFS), and distant metastasis&#x02010;free rates (DMFS). These findings indicate that prophylactic irradiation of the lower neck may be safely omitted in patients with CT/MRI&#x02010;diagnosed negative cervical LNs.</p><p>This trial provided preliminary evidence supporting the omission of lower neck irradiation in patients with LN&#x02010;negative disease. However, it had several limitations. First, it was a single&#x02010;center phase II trial. Additionally, many patients received two&#x02010;dimensional RT instead of contemporary intensity&#x02010;modulated RT (IMRT). The study did not address whether this de&#x02010;escalation approach is appropriate for patients with retropharyngeal LN involvement or stage N1 disease. Notably, patients with N0 disease comprised only a small proportion of the NPC population. However, several studies have expanded on these findings. In 2021, Chen et&#x000a0;al. conducted a phase II prospective study to investigate whether omitting levels IV and Vb is feasible in patients with N0&#x02010;1 NPC undergoing IMRT.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref> Elective neck irradiation included the retropharyngeal regions and levels II&#x02013;Va but excluded levels IV and Vb in patients with negative necks. Regarding nodal involvement, neck irradiation included the retropharyngeal areas and levels II&#x02013;Vb. This study enrolled 212 patients, including 128 with N0 and 84 with N1 disease. The findings revealed that omitting irradiation to levels IV and Vb in the initially negative necks resulted in a low rate of out&#x02010;of&#x02010;field nodal failure, with recurrence at these levels observed in only one patient (0.5%). Although not a randomized controlled trial, the study offers valuable insights into the potential omission of irradiation to levels IV and Vb in both unilateral and bilateral N0&#x02013;N1 cases.</p><p>Additionally, in 2021, a retrospective study compared selective irradiation of the upper neck (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;89) with WNI (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;30) in 119 patients with retropharyngeal LNs involvement only.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref> No significant difference in nodal recurrence (four in upper&#x02010;neck irradiation (UNI) vs. none in WNI) was observed between the groups. Another study also enrolled patients with NPC and retropharyngeal LNs only and indicated that irradiation of neck levels II, III, and VA might be feasible for this subgroup.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0033" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>33</sup>
</xref> Additionally, a retrospective study by Tang et&#x000a0;al. in 2017 evaluated more advanced cases, including patients with unilateral cervical LN metastases (N1, N3a, and N3b).<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>34</sup>
</xref> This study included 546 patients treated with IMRT, of whom 189 received irradiation limited to levels II&#x02013;Va in their negative necks (Group 1), while 357 patients received WNI (Group 2). They found that 22 patients developed cervical LN relapse, with 20 of 22 (91.0%) relapsing unilaterally within the pretreatment&#x02010;positive neck. No significant difference in nodal relapse&#x02010;free survival (NRFS) was observed between the two groups (96.1%&#x000a0;vs. 95.9%, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.94). These findings suggest that sparing the contralateral lower neck in the IMRT era is feasible for patients with NPC and unilateral cervical LN metastasis. However, as only 11.7 % (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;64) of patients had N3 disease, this conclusion should be approached with caution, particularly in patients with N3 disease.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0060"><label>6</label><title>ESTABLISHMENT OF UNINVOLVED LOWER NECK SPARING RT: RESULTS FROM RANDOMIZED TRIALS</title><p>Building on numerous retrospective studies, a large&#x02010;scale prospective trial in 2022 led by Sun et&#x000a0;al. evaluated 446 patients with NPC and N0&#x02013;N1 disease who were randomized to receive either UNI (levels II, III, and Va) alone (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;224) or WNI (levels II to V) (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;222) from three hospitals in China.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>35</sup>
</xref> All patients were staged using contrast&#x02010;enhanced MRI. After a median follow&#x02010;up of 53 months, 13 patients (3%) experienced regional nodal relapses (6 in the UNI vs. 7 in the WNI group), with no lower cervical LN relapses in the UNI group. The three&#x02010;year regional relapse&#x02010;free survival (RRFS) rates were similar between the two groups (UNI: 97.7%&#x000a0;vs. WNI: 96.3%). Both groups experienced similar acute toxicities, whereas UNI exhibited superior long&#x02010;terms outcomes, with lower incidences of hypothyroidism, dysphagia, skin toxicity, and soft tissue damage. These findings indicate that elective UNI of the uninvolved neck offers similar regional control with reduced radiation toxicities compared to standard WNI in patients with N0&#x02013;N1 NPC.</p><p>Extended follow&#x02010;up further highlighted the clinical benefits of UNI in patients with NPC. In 2024, the authors updated the five&#x02010;year survival outcomes, treatment&#x02010;related adverse events (TRAEs), and quality of life (QoL).<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0036" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>36</sup>
</xref> With a median follow&#x02010;up of 74 months, the UNI and WNI groups showed similar five&#x02010;year OS (95.9%&#x000a0;vs. 93.1%) and RRFS (95.0%&#x000a0;vs. 94.9%) rates. However, patients in the UNI group had significantly fewer delayed TRAEs than those in the WNI group (34%&#x000a0;vs. 48% for hypothyroidism, 29%&#x000a0;vs. 46% for neck tissue damage, 14%&#x000a0;vs. 27% for dysphagia, and 15%&#x000a0;vs. 26% for carotid artery stenosis). Patients in the UNI group had significantly better QoL outcomes, particularly regarding fatigue, emotional functioning, and swallowing. These phase III trial results strongly support treatment de&#x02010;escalation for early&#x02010;stage NPC. Long&#x02010;term follow&#x02010;up confirmed that UNI maintained survival outcomes, significantly reduced late toxicities, and improved the QoL. Consequently, UNI has gradually become accepted as a new standard of care for patients with N0&#x02013;N1 NPC (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1B</xref>).</p><p>A key contribution of this trial was its demonstration that treatment de&#x02010;escalation is safe for patients with N1 disease. However, for high&#x02010;risk patients, such as those with ipsilateral N3 (as classified by the eighth edition of the American Joint Committee on Cancer [AJCC]<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0037" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>37</sup>
</xref>), omitting irradiation of the contralateral neck requires further evaluation. In 2023, Tang et&#x000a0;al. evaluated the feasibility of UNI of the contralateral uninvolved neck in patients with unilateral N3.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0038" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>38</sup>
</xref> The study included 291 patients, with 190 receiving bilateral WNI (Group 1) and 101 undergoing WNI to the involved neck and UNI to the contralateral uninvolved neck (Group 2). After a median follow&#x02010;up of 79.4 months, regional LN relapse was observed in 25 patients (10.9% in Group 2, 11/101; and 7.4% in Group 1, 14/190). Among these relapses, 23 occurred within the previously involved neck regions, whereas only two relapses were observed in the contralateral uninvolved neck, with one case in each group. The five&#x02010;year RRFS rates were comparable between the two groups (89.7%&#x000a0;vs. 92.7%). This study suggests that, although N3 disease presents a high risk of treatment failure, the pattern of LN spread may be similar to that observed in other NPC cases. This finding indicates that skip or contralateral node metastases are rare. This preliminary evidence supports the possibility of omitting lower neck irradiation in the contralateral uninvolved neck in these patients. However, further high&#x02010;quality clinical trials are required to confirm these findings.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0070"><label>7</label><title>MEDIAL RETROPHARYNGEAL NODAL REGION SPARING RT</title><p>The retropharyngeal LN region, along with level II LNs, represents the first primary site of LN metastasis in NPC. Up to 70%&#x02013;86% of patients present with LN metastasis in the retropharyngeal region at the first diagnosis.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>39</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0040" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>40</sup>
</xref> These nodes, located in the suprahyoid portion of the retropharyngeal space, are categorized into medial and lateral subgroups. Previously, evaluating these nodes was challenging owing to technical limitations. For decades, complete coverage of both medial and lateral areas in RT has been the standard approach.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0041" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>41</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref> However, this approach results in high&#x02010;dose exposure to the oral cavity, pharyngeal constrictor muscles, and larynx, leading to oral mucositis and swallowing dysfunction. Recent studies have questioned this treatment approach to retropharyngeal LN metastasis. In 2006, a retrospective analysis of 275 patients with NPC who underwent MRI revealed a progressive decline in metastatic retropharyngeal LN incidence from the C1 to C3 levels, with rates of 62.5% for C1, 15.9% for C1/2, 10.8% for C2, 0.4% for C2/3, and 0.8% for C3.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref> In 2008, Tang et&#x000a0;al. reviewed 924 consecutive patients and reported that 73.5% presented with lateral retropharyngeal LN metastasis, whereas none had medial retropharyngeal LN metastasis.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>39</sup>
</xref> In another study conducted in 2014, Wang et&#x000a0;al. found that only 0.2% of 3,100 patients had LN in the medial area.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0040" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>40</sup>
</xref> These findings were also confirmed in a 2017 study investigating 10,651 LNs in 951 patients.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref> Overall, these data provide a rationale for excluding the medial region from the irradiation volumes.</p><p>Despite extensive LN analysis, clinical research assessing the impact of omitting medial retropharyngeal LN (MRLN) irradiation remains limited. As of 2022, only one study had assessed the possibility of omitting MRLNs irradiation.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref> Pan et&#x000a0;al. reported no significant differences in five&#x02010;year local relapse&#x02010;free survival (LRFS) (94.8%&#x000a0;vs. 94.2%) and RRFS (98.3%&#x000a0;vs. 97.1%) between the group with omission of MRLNs (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;183) and the group without omission (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;183). However, a significant reduction in the incidence of grade III acute oral mucositis was observed in the MRLN group.</p><p>Although a case&#x02010;control study, it serves as the basis for subsequent clinical trials. In 2023, the first randomized phase III trial launched by Mao et&#x000a0;al. addressed the unresolved issue of MRLN&#x02010;sparing RT. This study randomly assigned 568 patients with NPC into two groups: one group received MRLN&#x02010;sparing RT (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;285) and the other group received standard RT (n&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;283). Retropharyngeal LN recurrence was observed in six patients (1.1%), none of whom experienced relapse in the medial MRLN area. Intention&#x02010;to&#x02010;treat analysis showed comparable the three&#x02010;year LRFS between the MRLN&#x02010;sparing group and the control group (95.3%&#x000a0;vs. 95.5%, <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;0.95). Notably, patient&#x02010;reported outcomes assessed three years after sparing MRLN irradiation showed significant improvements across several domains, including global health status, role functioning, social functioning, fatigue, and swallowing, with improvements in swallowing reaching clinical significance. Overall, this study provides robust evidence to support the use of MRLN&#x02010;sparing RT in patients with non&#x02010;metastatic, non&#x02010;keratinizing NPC.</p><p>Sparing the MRLN during RT can improve patients' swallowing function owing to advancements in RT technology. IMRT precisely targets the required areas, allowing for minimal margins around target volumes. This reduces the irradiation range, ultimately resulting in a lower irradiation dose. However, in the two&#x02010;dimensional and three&#x02010;dimensional RT, decreasing irradiation volume of the medial retropharyngeal region is challenging. Therefore, we hypothesize that future application of more advanced conformal RT techniques, such as proton therapy, may further improve outcomes with MRLN sparing.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref> The same principle applies to lower neck&#x02010;sparing RT.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0080"><label>8</label><title>CURRENT AND FUTURE EXPLORE DIRECTIONS</title><sec id="pro670007-sec-0090"><label>8.1</label><title>Individualized neck RT</title><p>Two clinical studies conducted by the Sun Yat&#x02010;sen group have significantly increased interest in reducing the range of prophylactic neck irradiation in NPC. Minimizing the irradiation volume can directly decrease the exposure of normal tissues to radiation. However, there is growing interest in the effects of RT on immune function. Increasing evidence suggests that in programmed death&#x02010;1/programmed death&#x02010;ligand 1 (PD&#x02010;1/PD&#x02010;L1) checkpoint therapy, LNs in the tumor&#x02010;draining area are crucial sites for treatment responses. The normal microenvironment within LNs is linked to immunotherapy efficacy. Fransen et&#x000a0;al. reported that PD&#x02010;1 blockade induced higher immune activity in draining LNs than in non&#x02010;draining LNs, and that removal of draining LNs impaired immunotherapy efficacy.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref> In animal models of head and neck tumors, Laurel et&#x000a0;al. demonstrated that in cases that combined RT and immunotherapy, irradiation of the cervical LNs promoted tumor progression at primary and metastatic sites. LN irradiation significantly reduced both the tumor microenvironment and antigen&#x02010;specific T cells in mice, as well as &#x0201c;epitope spreading,&#x0201d; a marker of immune response.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref> These results suggested that extensive indiscriminate LN irradiation may reduce the efficacy of immunotherapy.</p><p>In this context,&#x000a0;individualized prophylactic neck irradiation approaches have emerged. Zhu et&#x000a0;al. analyzed 440 node&#x02010;positive NPC cases using PET/MRI and observed that the metastasis rate of level II&#x02013;V LNs decreased progressively from the C2 (66.5%) and C3 (68.2%) to T1 (4.1%) vertebral levels.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>49</sup>
</xref> Correlation analysis revealed that metastasis in Levels II&#x02013;V LNs at each vertebral level was associated with metastasis at other vertebral levels. Therefore, they proposed the use of vertebrae as landmarks to guide the delineation of neck CTVs in NPC. This approach limits prophylactic neck irradiation to the two cervical vertebrae below the lowest positive LN level. A prospective phase III clinical trial (NCT06241612) is currently evaluating patients with T1&#x02010;4N0&#x02010;2 or N3 category non&#x02010;bilateral lower LN metastasis. In this trail, the low&#x02010;risk neck CTV was limited to two vertebral levels below the level of metastatic involvement in levels II&#x02010;V, whereas the standard group received UNI at the uninvolved neck (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1C</xref>).</p><p>Liu et&#x000a0;al. manually contoured 5,836 positive LNs from 948 patients with NPC and calculated the Euclidean and vertical distances between each pair of LNs.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref> They observed that the maximum LN drainage distance was 3.95&#x000a0;cm when direction was not considered. In the head&#x02010;to&#x02010;foot direction, the vertical drainage distance was 2.81&#x000a0;cm. Consequently, they proposed that extending 3&#x000a0;cm caudally from the most inferior LN could be a reasonable lower border for prophylactic neck CTV. Similarly, a phase III clinical trial was launched to evaluate the feasibility of neck CTV, which included a 3&#x000a0;cm caudal extension below the transverse process of C1 for N0 disease and a 3&#x000a0;cm extension below the most inferior metastatic LN for cases with metastatic involvement (covering at least level II) (NCT05145660) (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1D</xref>).</p><p>Notably, this individualized approach to neck RT differs from the selective UNI strategy, which includes levels II to Va. The concept of selective LN levels irradiation is based on the international classification of cervical LNs, derived from the scope of surgical LN dissection.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>51</sup>
</xref> For example, the division between Levels II and III is based on the inferior margin of the hyoid bone, whereas Levels III and IV are based on the inferior margin of the cricoid cartilage. These divisions were based on landmark anatomical structures in the head&#x02010;to&#x02010;foot direction. However, these boundaries do not accurately reflect the anatomical scope of the lymphatic drainage pathways. Acknowledging this limitation, researchers have begun to address the limitations of this anatomical division in NPC, such as using the distance between positive LNs.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0100"><label>8.2</label><title>Visible LN irradiation in lower risk levels</title><p>For HNSCC, some studies have proposed excluding neck CTV, focusing solely on LN irradiation. Some authors hypothesize that modern radiology and histology can detect all macroscopic tumors at the LN level, with occult metastases more likely to occur in the LNs rather than in the surrounding fatty tissue.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> Therefore, it may be feasible to target radiation to the involved and suspicious LNs instead of irradiating the entire LN. In 2023, Sher et&#x000a0;al. reported preliminary findings from a phase II trial (INRT&#x02010;AIR),<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0053" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>53</sup>
</xref> where only positive and suspicious LNs were irradiated in HNSCC, omitting traditional neck CTV (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1E</xref>). After a median follow&#x02010;up of 33.4 months, no patient presented with neck recurrence. In the same year, Reinders et&#x000a0;al. introduced a similar clinical protocol (iNode),<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> which also shifted neck CTV irradiation to selectively target involved and suspicious LNs.</p><p>However, this approach is less aggressive in NPC. By contrast, efforts have focused on omitting irradiation of lower&#x02010;risk neck CTVs. In NPC, the retropharyngeal areas and level II are generally considered high&#x02010;risk regions regardless of the N category and must be included in the neck CTV. The risk level at other LN levels is determined based on the status of LN metastasis. For example, inferior levels following regions with positive LNs are often considered low&#x02010;risk areas. Hence, based on selective UNI irradiation, our team proposed the concept of visible LN irradiation at low&#x02010;risk neck levels in NPC and launched a prospective single&#x02010;arm phase I study (ChiCTR2300067783). In this study, we abandoned irradiation of lower&#x02010;risk LN levels based on the LN metastasis status instead targeting the visible LNs at these levels (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1F</xref>) (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="pro670007-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). To date, 20 patients have been enrolled in this study. With a median follow&#x02010;up of 11.6 months (range: 1.5&#x02013;20.3 months), no cases of neck recurrence have been observed; however, longer follow&#x02010;up is needed to confirm these findings.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="pro670007-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Visible lymph node irradiation (VNRT) of the low&#x02010;risk lymph node levels.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AJCC8<sup>th</sup>
</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Region of the positive LN</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Range of prophylactic neck irradiation</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Visible lymph node irradiation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02013;</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bilateral levels II</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bilateral levels III and Va</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Retropharyngeal LN only</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bilateral levels II</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bilateral levels III and Va</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">LNs in level II</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bilateral levels II+ ipsilateral neck levels III and Va</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ipsilateral neck levels IV and Vb, and contralateral levels III and Va.</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">LNs in level II, III and Va</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bilateral levels II+ ipsilateral neck levels III, IV and V</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">contralateral levels III and Va</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="pro670007-tbl1-note-0001"><p>
<italic toggle="yes">Note</italic>: Visible lymph nodes are defined as lymph nodes with a maximum diameter of at least 3&#x000a0;mm, but not meeting the criteria for positive lymph nodes.</p></fn><fn id="pro670007-tbl1-note-0002"><p>Abbreviation: LN, lymph node.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><permissions><copyright-holder>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons, Ltd.</copyright-holder></permissions></table-wrap></sec></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0110"><label>9</label><title>CONCLUSION</title><p>This review outlines the development of prophylactic neck irradiation in NPC, from early bilateral WNI to approaches that spare irradiation of the uninvolved lower neck and medial retropharyngeal nodal region, leading to the current exploration of individualized prophylactic irradiation guided by LN and vertebral distances. Changes in the concept and scope of prophylactic neck irradiation stem from a deeper understanding of the metastatic patterns and recurrence risks of LN in NPC. Overall, these innovations represent a promising evolution in NPC management, offering therapeutic options that prioritize both efficacy and patient well&#x02010;being. Future developments in deep learning and radiomics may enable precise prediction of LN metastasis and its spread to surrounding nodes.<xref rid="pro670007-bib-0054" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>54</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="pro670007-bib-0055" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>55</sup>
</xref> This could lead to the development of prophylactic neck irradiation targeting only single LNs. Additionally, personalized radiation doses could be tailored based on the metastasis risk of each node, significantly reducing irradiation volume (such as the total abandonment of the CTV) and lowering radiation exposure to the neck.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0120"><title>AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS</title><p>Wenjun Liao and Yue Zhao, analysis, interpretation of data, and drafting the article; Shichuan Zhang, conception and design the study, revising the draft. All authors read and approved the final manuscript.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="pro670007-sec-0140"><title>CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT</title><p>The authors have no competing interests.</p></sec><sec id="pro670007-sec-0170"><title>ETHICS APPROVAL</title><p>Not applicable</p></sec></body><back><ack id="pro670007-sec-0130"><title>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS</title><p>This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant 82203197.</p></ack><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="pro670007-sec-0160"><title>DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT</title><p>Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.</p></sec><ref-list id="pro670007-bibl-0001"><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="pro670007-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="pro670007-cite-0002">
